 & =s\\cdot \\frac{2s-3}{{s}^{2}-3s}-2 \\\\  & =\\frac{s(2s-3)-2({s}^{2}-3s)}{{s}^{2}-3s} \\\\  & =\\frac{3s}{s(s-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{s-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-64324/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "51f6b682-ce7e-5068-8679-06a452eed647"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-64324"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-64323"] :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform of ${f}^{′}(t)$ for $s>3$ given that the function $f(t)$ is continuous over $[0,\\infty ),$ $f(0)=2,$ and\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=\\frac{2s-1}{{s}^{2}-s-6},\\,s>3.\n$$\n\nAssume that ${f}^{′}$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $F(s)=L{f(t)}$ where $f(t)$ is a continuous function of exponential order $a$ on $[0,\\infty ).$ If ${f}^{′}(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty ),$ then\n\n$$\nL{{f}^{′}(t)}=sL{f(t)}-f(0).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the Laplace transform of ${f}^{′}(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{f}^{′}(t)} & =sL{f(t)}-f(0) \\\\  & =s\\cdot \\frac{2s-1}{{s}^{2}-s-6}-2 \\\\  & =\\frac{s(2s-1)-2({s}^{2}-s-6)}{{s}^{2}-s-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{s+12}{{s}^{2}-s-6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-89453/selection/2048688caf5ac66a8c0d40847881fb5c21033717918a32a93b3ce928174ae91a" :answer/id #uuid "09ee2386-0388-5687-ad86-95279be4bc4e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89453/selection/d5dc5c8ee61bcb233790afcdadeccc746d49dbff3b334c244dbc95d28a8f2985" :answer/id #uuid "19b38561-769a-5abe-9ed9-17a38ee32262" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2s+1}{s-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89453/selection/a3f399bf4023b46028bdbd9e356582247751a09b8a289fbb9345c08f6535dd5a" :answer/id #uuid